  "response": "Adjudication between the two accounts turns less on theory than on which body of evidence is allowed to discipline the narrative, and the monetary-policy account begins with the rate path of the early 2000s. The Federal Reserve lowered its federal funds rate target to 1 percent in June 2003; that cut is what shows John Taylor to have been right in arguing that the Federal Reserve held the federal funds rate below the level prescribed by the Taylor rule from 2002 to 2005. That is, the policy rate was taken down to zero percent in 2003. Whereas Taylor locates the crisis impulse in a central bank that drifted from its own rule, Spain and Ireland experienced large housing booms under European Central Bank monetary policy. That cross-country pattern is widely held to confirm Ben Bernanke's attribution of much of the rise in United States house prices to a global saving glut of foreign capital inflows.\n\nThe second line of adjudication abandons aggregates for disaggregated lending records, where the operative question becomes who received credit and on what terms. Atif Mian and Amir Sufi found that mortgage credit expanded fastest in ZIP codes populated by borrowers with low credit scores and flat incomes, and that gradient in credit growth confirms that subprime mortgages accounted for roughly 20 percent of United States mortgage originations in 2006. The FCIC majority report identified widespread failures in financial regulation and supervision.\n\nStatutory history supplies a third strand of the argument. The Commodity Futures Modernization Act of 2000 exempted most over-the-counter derivatives from federal regulation. The Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act repealed the Glass-Steagall separation of commercial and investment banking in 2007, and that dismantling of the statutory firewall indicates just how extensive the regulatory and supervisory failures catalogued by the commission had become.\n\nThe commission's own framing, however, is not free of internal tension. The FCIC majority report concluded that Federal Reserve interest rate policy was the single principal cause of the crisis, and that verdict cannot be squared with the majority's ultimate conclusion that the crisis resulted from failures in regulation and supervision rather than from Federal Reserve interest rate policy, which is to say with the very breakdown of oversight the report had identified. The Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission released its final report in January 2011, a full decade after the Commodity Futures Modernization Act was enacted. On balance, the credit-allocation evidence has proved the more decisive for most economists, since it ties the deterioration in loan quality to particular institutions, instruments and supervisory gaps rather than to the level of a single policy rate.",
